            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Arabic for to hire, then the Arabic for a backpack."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: سكة حديد",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "سكة حديد — sikkat ḥadīd — \"a railway\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "وصلت سكة الحديد إلى قريتنا قبل قرن. waṣalat sikkat al-ḥadīd ilā qaryatinā qabla qarn. The railway reached our village a century ago."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "سكة حديد (sikkat ḥadīd) is built from two native Arabic words: sikka, a straight road or row, on the root s-k-k (Lane), and ḥadīd, \"iron\", on the root ḥ-d-d (Lane): a road of iron."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for getting around."
            }
          ]
        },
